"The patient underwent a robot-assisted radical prostatectomy for clinically significant prostate cancer, with a preoperative PSA of 160. The surgical pathology revealed a Gleason 4+3 with tertiary pattern 5 (ISUP grade group 3) and pathological stage pT4, with extraprostatic extension and positive surgical margins. The seminal vesicles were not invaded, and lymphovascular invasion was present, but there was no lymph node metastasis documented. Biopsy findings showed a Gleason 3+4 (ISUP grade group 2).\n\nTwo primary factors drive the prognosis: the high pathological stage (pT4) and positive surgical margins. Countervailing findings include the absence of lymph node metastasis and the non-invasion of the seminal vesicles. The difference between the biopsy grade (Gleason 3+4) and the final resection grade (Gleason 4+3) is noted. Missing postoperative PSA or follow-up data currently widens the uncertainty regarding the subsequent trajectory. Information regarding postoperative PSA and its subsequent trajectory is crucial for refining the prognosis. The fixed estimated horizon is 27.15 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 21.72–32.58 months; its coverage has not been validated."
